In this lesson we'll look at the central difference approximation, a symmetric way to estimate the derivative of a function. Instead of using points on only one side of where you're interested in, you grab a value a small step to the left and a small step to the right. So you look at the function at x minus h and at x plus h, then you take the difference between those two values and divide by the total width of the interval, which is two times h. Why it is "central" is because you're drawing a chord that sits right in the middle of x, rather than leaning to one side. It's the slope of the line connecting the point on the left to the point on the right, and it naturally balances out any one-sided bias. Graphically, picture a smooth curve. Choose any point x, then draw a chord that stretches equally to the left and to the right. The slope of that chord is your estimate of the derivative at x. As you shrink the chord, making h smaller and smaller, that estimate gets closer and closer to the true tangent gradient. Now let's see it in action.